 ## Background
-The Marching Cubes algorithm was introduced by Lorensen & Cline in 1987 [S3, unofficial]. It is a general approach covering a whole family of algorithms, some of which are adaptive, supplemented with sharp feature detection, or capable of preserving the topology of non-manifold surfaces [S1, S5, unofficial]. Conceptually, simple contouring covers the plane with a grid and checks if the function changes sign between the centers of adjacent cells; if it does, the shared border becomes a piece of the contour [S1, S6, unofficial]. Marching Cubes improves upon this by measuring function signs in every corner for each square, adding line segments that cross the cells, and connecting sign-changing edges [S1, S6, unofficial]. Since pairs of signs for each shared edge change synchronously for both cells, the line segments connect automatically into a continuous contour [S1, S6, unofficial]. Linear interpolation is used to find the exact position of the line segment ends on the edges, rather than placing them at the middle, though this is an approximation because the target function is not necessarily linear [S1, S6, unofficial]. The error of the simple contour (middle-to-middle) never exceeds half of the cell's diagonal [S1, S5, unofficial].
+The Marching Cubes algorithm was introduced by Lorensen & Cline in 1987 [S4, unofficial]. It is a general approach covering a whole family of algorithms, some of which are adaptive, supplemented with sharp feature detection, or capable of preserving the topology of non-manifold surfaces [S1, S5, unofficial]. Conceptually, simple contouring covers the plane with a grid and checks if the function changes sign between the centers of adjacent cells; if it does, the shared border becomes a piece of the contour [S1, S6, unofficial]. Marching Cubes improves upon this by measuring function signs in every corner for each square, adding line segments that cross the cells, and connecting sign-changing edges [S1, S6, unofficial]. Since pairs of signs for each shared edge change synchronously for both cells, the line segments connect automatically into a continuous contour [S1, S6, unofficial]. Linear interpolation is used to find the exact position of the line segment ends on the edges, rather than placing them at the middle, though this is an approximation because the target function is not necessarily linear [S1, S6, unofficial]. The error of the simple contour (middle-to-middle) never exceeds half of the cell's diagonal [S1, S5, unofficial].
 
